export const OPENERS_BIRDS = {
  "birds/alexandrine-parakeet": [
    "Psittacula eupatria is the Alexandrine parakeet, a South Asian parrot listed at 23 inches and 8 to 10 ounces. Anyone comparing it with an Indian ringneck is looking at a smaller relative. The Alexandrine is the larger bird, with a heavier beak and a house-filling call. The listed noise level is loud, and talking ability is very good, so clear words and a dawn scream can belong to the same animal. The temperament line is independent, gentle, and intelligent. Gentle applies to a bird that was handled with patience. Independent means an empty day produces its own, usually loud, solution.",
    "Adult males often show a black neck ring and a pink collar. That plumage marks sex in a mature male. It is not a health score, and a young bird in a shop may not have grown it yet. Hens and immature males can look plain around the neck without being a different species. Both sexes need the same roomy cage, the same diet, and time out of the cage every day. Buying a juvenile only for the collar misses the adult voice, which arrives on its own schedule.",
    "A 23-inch parakeet needs a cage that lets the tail clear the bars while the bird climbs and turns. Budgie housing is the wrong object, and a decorative dome is worse. Daily time outside the cage, in a room with the door closed and the ceiling fan off, is part of basic care. Nonstick fumes, open windows, and other pets change that free time from exercise into an accident. The beak will test latches, picture frames, and any perch thin enough to splinter.",
    "Lifespan is 25 to 30 years. Talking practice is short repetition of ordinary words, not a shouting match with the contact call. Answering every scream teaches the bird that volume works. Thin apartment walls and a neighbor on the other side of them are part of the decision. A person who wants a quiet evening should listen to an adult Alexandrine before treating the talking score as the only fact on the card. The loud rating does not expire after the first phrases are learned.",
    "Pellets and vegetables should be the ordinary meal, with seed and nuts measured rather than left in a hopper. Sunflower as the whole diet is a common path to vitamin A shortage, dull feathers, and respiratory irritation. Weigh an 8 to 10 ounce bird on a gram scale when the look of the chest changes. A sudden drop, or a bird that sits heavy and stops climbing, belongs with an avian veterinarian. New arrivals also raise the usual questions about hygiene between cages and about psittacine beak and feather disease.",
    "Initial setup on this card runs from a few hundred dollars into the thousands, mostly because a cage that survives this beak is not a budgie starter kit. Annual food, veterinary care, and toys continue for the full 25 to 30 years. Spring hormones can make a previously easy bird territorial toward one person in the house. That shift is seasonal behavior in a large Psittacula, handled with a calmer routine and less shoulder time, not with punishment. Captive-bred birds from a source that will discuss diet and parentage are the birds to seek.",
  ],

  "birds/american-singer-canary": [
    "The American Singer is a song-canary breed developed in the United States from roller and border-type birds. It is a domestic canary, about 5 inches long, with a listed lifespan of 10 to 15 years. It is not a color breed, and it is not a wild Atlantic canary. Serinus canaria, the island finch, is the ancestor. This page is the American breed kept for its song. Judges score that song. A yellow pet-shop bird chosen because the feathers looked bright is a different project from a bird whose family was selected for voice.",
    "Noise is moderate and melodious. That phrase means a male's song, not a parrot contact scream and not silence. Care level is beginner, which is fair only when someone will learn the song calendar and the cage routine. The diet on the card is seeds, greens, and egg food. A seed stick alone leaves a gap. Egg food matters more in a molt and when a hen is breeding, and greens have to be washed and removed before they sour. Water cups in a 5-inch bird's cage foul quickly.",
    "Housing starts at 24 by 12 by 18 inches, and a longer flight cage is the better song room. Horizontal space lets the singer hop and flutter between perches. A tall narrow cage with one swing is a poor song studio. Several perch diameters protect the feet. The cage belongs out of drafts, out of the kitchen, and away from a constantly running television if the point of the breed is a clean song. Canaries are not shoulder pets. Handling is a stress for most of them, and the relationship is listening and husbandry.",
    "Song training is repetition and example, often with a tutor recording or an adult male, during the season when the bird is already inclined to sing. Forcing noise at a bird that is quiet, fluffed, or molting does not build a better voice. A quiet night matters. Cover or dim the cage so the singer gets a long uninterrupted dark period. Household clatter through the late evening leaves a bird that rehearses at the wrong hour and rests badly. Hens are not the show singers. Expect the sustained song from the male.",
    "Ten to 15 years is long enough for several molt cycles and for the song to change as the bird ages. A young male in full voice and a seven-year-old with a shorter phrase can both be healthy. Weight loss, a soiled vent, or a bird that stops eating are the changes that need a clinician who sees small birds. Ordinary dog-and-cat clinics often decline canaries. Find that person before the first quiet week, because a 5-inch bird has very little reserve.",
    "This breed's reputation sits on the song score, not on a photograph. Nothing here depends on a particular Commons image or on a color name. Type, stance, and the way the bird delivers its tours are what a song judge is listening and looking for. A keeper who wants red feathers should be reading the red-factor page. A keeper who wants speech should not be buying a canary at all. American Singers do not mimic human talk. They sing, and the household has to be willing to let that song happen in daylight and stop at night.",
  ],

  "birds/cape-parrot": [
    "The Cape parrot is Poicephalus robustus, a South African parrot listed at 13 inches and 10 to 13 ounces. Senegal parrots and Meyer's parrots are smaller birds in the same genus, with different ranges and a different job as pets. A Cape is not the small beginner Poicephalus. The noise rating is moderate, and talking ability is good. Temperament on the card is gentle, playful, and intelligent, which still leaves a beak that can split a hardwood perch and a personality that notices sloppy handling.",
    "Wild Cape parrots are endangered. A pet on this page should be a captive-bred bird with a documented source, not an imported adult of unclear origin. South Africa is the origin line for a reason. The species is tied to a limited forest range and to particular foods in that range, and captive birds still do better when the diet is varied instead of reduced to one seed mix. Pellets, vegetables, and a measured nut portion fit a 10 to 13 ounce parrot better than sunflower alone.",
    "Lifespan is 30 to 60 years. That spread is wide because husbandry decides which end a bird reaches. A Cape acquired in a person's thirties can still be alive when that person is thinking about retirement. The moderate noise rating is not a whisper. It is quieter than a macaw and still too much bird for a studio apartment that wanted a Senegal. Good talking means words are a realistic hope. It does not replace foraging, bathing, and time out of the cage.",
    "Housing has to match a stocky 13-inch Poicephalus, not a cockatiel. The bird needs room to climb, a bath, and toys that survive a beak built for hard food. Playful on the temperament line shows up as shredding and as investigating hardware. Intelligent shows up as boredom if the only object in the cage is a swing. An avian veterinarian who has seen large Poicephalus patients is worth finding before the first week, because 30 to 60 years is a long time to improvise clinics.",
    "People shopping for a gentle African parrot sometimes land on a Cape because the genus name looks familiar. The weight tells the difference. Ten to 13 ounces is a solid bird, and the endangered status means the purchase is also a conservation question. Ask what the parents ate, how the chick was raised, and whether the seller can explain why this is robustus rather than a smaller relative. A varied diet from the start is easier than converting a seed-addicted adult decades into a 30 to 60 year life.",
  ],

  "birds/cordon-bleu": [
    "The cordon-bleu is a waxbill finch in the genus Uraeginthus, not a parrot. The card lists it as very small, about 5 inches, with a lifespan of 7 to 10 years. Noise is low, a soft warble. Care level is intermediate. These birds are kept to watch, as a pair or a small group. They are not candidates for shoulder time, talking lessons, or a mirror and a bell sold as parrot enrichment. A single cordon-bleu in a living-room cage is a social mistake.",
    "In the common species, males show the blue belly and the red cheek. That pattern is how keepers sex a mature male of the red-cheeked bird. Hens lack the red cheek patch, and young birds can be ambiguous, so a shop label is not the last word. Other Uraeginthus species do not wear the same face. Buying a group of mixed waxbills and calling all of them cordon-bleus produces fights and a breeding result nobody planned. House compatible birds, and give them enough length of cage to pass one another.",
    "The diet is seeds, live insects, and egg food. A dry finch mix is the base, not the whole menu. Small live foods and egg food supply the protein these waxbills use, especially when they are breeding or molting. Greens should be fresh and brief, then removed. Avocado does not belong in the cage, not as a treat and not as a leftover from a human plate. The same rule covers chocolate, caffeine, and anything salted. A 5-inch bird cannot afford a toxic experiment.",
    "Give the waxbills at least 24 by 12 by 18 inches, and prefer a flight cage to a tall box. Drafts are the other half of housing. A window cracked behind the cage, or an air vent aimed at the perches, chills birds this small. Keep the flight out of the kitchen and out of direct midday sun through glass. Bar spacing has to be narrow enough that a waxbill cannot slip through. Cover at one end lets the group retreat when the room is busy. Open wire on all sides, in a hallway, is a stressful address.",
    "Seven to 10 years is the lifespan to plan, which is longer than people assume when the bird was inexpensive. Intermediate care means the pair, the insects, the temperature, and the daily look at droppings, not a complicated training program. A soft warble is the normal voice. A bird that goes silent, sits low, or breathes with an open beak needs a veterinarian who treats finches, the same day. These birds decline quickly, and a dog-and-cat clinic may not be equipped for a 5-inch patient.",
    "Eggs and extra males are the management problem in a group that seemed peaceful in the store. Provide a nest only if you are prepared for chicks, and separate birds that start driving others off the perches. Live insects should be gut-loaded and sized for a waxbill, not dumped in as a pile of large worms. Egg food spoils in heat and should be offered in a small dish that is washed out. The soft warble, the blue belly, and the red cheek are the pleasure of the common male. The work is the flock, the diet, and a cage that does not leak cold air.",
  ],

  "birds/dusky-conure": [
    "The dusky conure is Aratinga weddellii, also placed in Eupsittula, a South American parrot of about 10 inches and 3.5 to 4 ounces. It is smaller than a sun conure and quieter than a sun conure. It is still a parrot. Noise on the card is moderate, and the bird still screams at dawn and dusk. Speech is listed as limited. A few words are a bonus. The contact call is the voice you will actually live with. Temperament is listed as playful, cuddly, and social, which describes a bird that wants company, not a silent lap ornament.",
    "Social, for this species, means a flock or a person who is home. A dusky left alone in a covered cage for a workweek becomes a screamer, a plucker, or both. Cuddly shows up after trust, as a bird that wants to be near someone, and it does not cancel the dusk call. Playful means shredded paper, chewed toys, and an interest in anything left on top of the cage. Ten inches is compact next to a macaw and still too much animal for a travel carrier used as housing.",
    "Lifespan is 20 to 30 years. That is long enough that the moderate noise has to be acceptable in the actual rooms where people sleep and take calls. An apartment that needed a quiet pet will not become one because this conure is quieter than a sun. Neighbors hear dawn. Training the volume down works only at the margins. You can teach a step-up and a foraging routine. You cannot delete a flock call from an Eupsittula.",
    "Limited talking is a reason to buy the bird for the green-brown plumage and the companionship, not for a vocabulary. The dusky head is the field mark people notice once the sun-conure colors are set aside. Diet should be pellets and vegetables, with fruit and a little seed as extras. A seed-only bowl puts weight on a 3.5 to 4 ounce frame and leaves vitamin gaps. Bath water, destructible toys, and a few hours out of the cage in a safe room are the daily shape of care.",
    "Twenty to 30 years also means planning a caregiver. A playful bird that is cuddly with one person may bite another when hormones rise, which is ordinary conure behavior and a poor surprise for a child. Watch appetite and droppings, and treat a fluffed, silent dusky as sick. The loud one at dusk is the healthy pattern. Captive-bred birds are the ethical and practical purchase. South America is the origin, not an invitation to treat a wild-caught adult as a starter pet.",
  ],

  "birds/finch": [
    "This page covers small finches, not one species and not a parrot. The scientific line is various Taeniopygia, Lonchura, and Erythrura species. Origin is Australia, Africa, or Asia depending on which bird is in the cage. Size is tiny, about 3 to 6 inches and 10 to 30 grams. Lifespan is 5 to 10 years, and up to 15 for some species. Noise is low, pleasant chirps and peeps. Talking ability is none. These birds do not mimic speech. Anyone shopping for a talking companion is in the wrong section of the site.",
    "Zebra finches, Taeniopygia, are the hardy colony bird. They are the usual beginner finch, active, ready to breed, and tolerant of ordinary careful husbandry. Society finches, a domestic Lonchura, can foster. Keepers use them to rear the eggs and chicks of species that fail as parents. That talent is not a reason to dump every abandoned egg on one pair, and it is not a personality transplant. A society finch is still a small bird with a short life, and it does not want to be held.",
    "Gouldian finches, Erythrura, are the fragile birds on this page. The colors are real, and so is the narrower margin for cold, stress, and dirty air. A gouldian that was sold beside a zebra finch does not share the zebra's toughness. Warmth, a stable room, and a keeper who already understands finch illness matter more here than they do for a zebra colony. Mixing the two without a plan is how a hardy flock hides the fact that the colorful birds are failing.",
    "A flight cage at least 30 inches long is the space line, and longer is better. Finches fly horizontally. A tall parrot cage with a bell teaches nothing useful and invites a collision. Bar spacing has to hold a 10-gram bird. The diet is a finch seed mix, millet, vegetables, and egg food. Live food helps some species when they are breeding. Seed piled in a hopper, with no greens and no egg food, is a thin menu across a 5 to 10 year life, or across the longer span some species reach.",
    "Care is listed as beginner-friendly. Read that as a comment on zebra and society finches kept as a group, with a proper flight and a simple diet. It is a poor description of a single gouldian, and it is a poor description of any finch kept alone. These are flock birds. One finch on a swing is a welfare problem, not a minimalist pet. Pairs and small groups let them preen, roost, and call. Human interaction is watching and cleaning, not scratching a head.",
    "Because the page is a group, the receipt has to name the bird. A 3-inch waxbill and a 6-inch Java finch do not share a bar spacing or a risk of being bullied. Up to 15 years for some species means you cannot budget every finch as a five-year decoration. Chirps and peeps stay pleasant only when the group is healthy and the cage is long enough to prevent constant chasing. No amount of talking practice will produce a word. The pleasure is the colony, the flight, and the differences among zebra, society, gouldian, and the similar small finches kept with them.",
  ],

  "birds/golden-conure": [
    "The golden conure, also called the Queen of Bavaria conure, is Guaruba guarouba. It is a Brazilian parrot listed at 14 inches and 8 to 10 ounces. The yellow plumage is the reason people stop at the cage. The species behind that color is a large, social, loud conure with a beak to match the body. Origin is Brazil. Temperament on the card is affectionate, social, and playful. Those words describe a flock bird that wants contact, wrecks toys, and objects to being left as a beautiful ornament.",
    "Wild golden conures are endangered, and the species is on CITES Appendix I. That listing is a legal fact, not a slogan. A pet should be captive-bred, with paperwork a buyer can read, from a source that can explain the birds in the pedigree. An inexpensive yellow conure at a general pet shop is the wrong story for this species. Appendix I animals are not impulse stock. If the seller cannot discuss permits, parentage, and diet, the bird should stay where it is.",
    "The lifespan line is 20 to 30 years. The card does not offer a short trial period hidden inside the color. A person who takes a golden conure home is planning a multi-decade flock member with a loud voice and a powerful bite. Affectionate does not mean safe around faces, children, or visitors who want to poke the yellow feathers. Social means the bird suffers when the house is empty. Playful means wood, leather, and drywall are all candidate toys unless the room is arranged otherwise.",
    "The noise rating for a golden conure is loud. A 14-inch bird calls like a parrot that expects a distant flock to answer. Apartment walls do not absorb that. Training can teach stationing and a few cues. It does not turn the species quiet. People who came from a green-cheek conure, or from a cockatiel, misread the size and the volume. Eight to 10 ounces is a solid bird, closer to a small Amazon in presence than to the conures sold as starter pets. The loud rating belongs in the decision before the purchase, while the yellow is still behind someone else's glass.",
    "The talking rating is moderate. Some individuals learn words and short phrases. Many do not become conversational, and none of them become quiet in exchange for a vocabulary. Buy the species for the flock bond and the play, and treat words as optional. Repeating phrases during a calm part of the day works better than shouting over a contact call. A golden that screams and then hears laughter or an immediate release from the cage will keep the scream. The moderate talking score is not a volume control.",
    "The cage has to be large, or the bird needs an aviary. A 14-inch parrot with an 8 to 10 ounce body and a long tail cannot live in a cockatiel flight and stay sound. Width for the wings, strong bars, and a play stand outside the cage are the minimum shape of housing. Time out of the cage is daily, in a room that has been stripped of toxic metals, open water, and fumes. Playful birds investigate the stove. Affectionate birds follow the person into unsafe rooms. Door discipline is part of care.",
    "Diet is a parrot diet with enough variety for a bird this size: a formulated pellet, vegetables, some fruit, and nuts as a measured portion rather than a bowl of sunflower. A golden conure will gladly eat the fatty items first. Weight should be watched, because 8 to 10 ounces is the healthy band, not a suggestion to fatten a pet-shop bird that already looks round. Foraging toys slow the meal down and give a social, intelligent animal something to do when nobody is standing at the cage.",
    "The beak can break skin. A bite from a startled or hormonal Guaruba is an injury, not a pinch from a parrotlet. Handle with a plan for step-up, a neutral perch, and a household that agrees on the rules. Children are not the trainers. Visitors do not get to put a finger through the bars of an Appendix I parrot because the color is pretty. Affection runs toward the person who does the work. It does not run toward every face in a crowd, and it can reverse during breeding condition.",
    "Annual food, toys, and veterinary care continue across the 20 to 30 year lifespan, and the first cage is the expensive object people underbuy. A golden conure that plucks, screams for hours, or lunges has often spent a year in a cage that was too small with a diet that was mostly seed. Fixing that later is harder than buying the right setup first. An avian veterinarian who will see a rare conure should be chosen before the bird arrives. Endangered status does not come with a special immunity to ordinary parrot illness.",
    "Brazil is the wild origin, and captive birds still behave like flock parrots from that forest, not like cage ornaments. A legal, captive-bred Queen of Bavaria is a serious companion for a household that can stand loud calls, a large enclosure, and decades of attention. It is not a pet-shop conure. The yellow feathers are the advertisement. The animal is 14 inches, 8 to 10 ounces, loud, playful, and protected. Anyone who only wanted the color has not yet met the species.",
  ],

  "birds/half-moon-conure": [
    "The half-moon conure is the orange-fronted conure, Eupsittula canicularis, from Central America. Older labels still say Aratinga canicularis. The half-moon name comes from the orange band on the forehead. This is a smaller conure, listed at 9 to 10 inches and 2.5 to 3 ounces, and it is still noisy. Noise on the card is moderate. That is quieter than a sun or a golden, and it is not a quiet parakeet. The talking entry is limited. Temperament is playful, social, and gentle.",
    "Central America is the origin, and wild populations have been pressured by capture and habitat loss. A pet should be captive-bred. A wild-caught adult that bites, hides, and will not eat pellets is a harder project than a hand-raised youngster, and it is the wrong bird to learn on. Nine to 10 inches looks manageable in a shop cage. At home the same bird needs flight, not a perch it never leaves. Gentle on the card assumes a familiar person and a predictable routine.",
    "Plan on 20 to 30 years. A word or two is possible, and a conversation is not the plan. The voice that fills those decades is the conure call, especially when the bird wants the flock. Social birds kept in a silent room all day get louder, not calmer. A half-moon can live with a human flock if someone is actually home, or with a compatible partner if the keeper understands pairing. One bird plus a television is not a flock.",
    "Flight is a requirement, not an enrichment extra. A 2.5 to 3 ounce conure is built to move. Clipped wings and a small round cage produce a bird that climbs one wall of the bars and screams. A flight cage with horizontal space, or a safe room where the bird can fly each day, matches the body. Windows, fans, and other pets are the hazards during that time. Playful on the temperament line shows up as aerobatics and as an interest in anything that swings or shreds.",
    "The diet has to be varied. Pellets, vegetables, some fruit, and a limited seed portion cover a bird that will otherwise live on millet and sunflower. At 2.5 to 3 ounces, extra fat shows up quickly, and a seed-only body is a poor candidate for a 20 to 30 year life. Offer food in more than one place so a social pair, or a single bird that is food-aggressive, still eats. Remove fresh food before it spoils. Clean water is a daily task because conures dunk pellets and bathe in bowls.",
    "Moderate noise still means dawn and dusk calls that a neighbor can hear. People buy the orange forehead and the manageable length, then discover the voice. Training helps with step-up and with occupying the beak. It will not delete flock calling from Eupsittula canicularis. If the household needs library quiet, this is the wrong species even though the card does not say extremely high. Compare it with a sun conure and it is the calmer bird. Compare it with a finch and it is a parrot.",
    "Toys have to be destructible and replaceable. A half-moon that cannot shred will shred the cage accessories that were supposed to be permanent. Foot toys, paper, soft wood, and foraging cups fit a playful 9 to 10 inch conure. Rotate them before the bird finishes every one and starts on the windowsill. Gentle birds still nip when they are hormonal, tired, or guarding a person. A bite from this species is smaller than a golden conure's and still unpleasant enough to teach bad habits if someone jerks the hand away and squeals.",
    "Out-of-cage time is daily. Social and playful birds left in the cage while the household lives in another room become contact-callers. A play stand in the room where people actually sit works better than an hour of guilty attention at midnight. Shoulders are optional and often a bad habit, because a startled bird on a shoulder bites a face. Use a perch and a hand that the bird has already practiced stepping onto. Twenty to 30 years is a long time to undo a biting routine that started in month two.",
    "Limited speech is not a training failure. Repeating one short word, in a quiet moment, is as far as many half-moons go. The orange frontal band, the green body, and the flock voice are the species. Half-moon, orange-fronted, and Petz's are names for this same bird, Eupsittula canicularis. A jenday or a sun conure is a different animal with a different voice. Ask for canicularis on the receipt before you plan a 20 to 30 year diet and flight setup around the wrong conure.",
    "A varied diet and real flight are the two husbandry points people skip after they notice the bird is smaller than a sun. Smaller does not mean budgie care. Nine to 10 inches and 2.5 to 3 ounces still needs a parrot cage, parrot food, and a person or a flock. Central America produced a bird that calls. Captive life of 20 to 30 years asks the household to answer that with routine, not with a blanket over the cage every time the telephone rings. Moderate noise is the rating. A life with no flight is how that rating gets worse.",
  ],

  "birds/hawk-headed-parrot": [
    "The hawk-headed parrot is Deroptyus accipitrinus, also called the red-fan parrot, from South America. The card lists 12 to 14 inches and 6 to 10 ounces, a lifespan of 30 to 40 years, noise that is moderate to loud, and moderate talking ability. Temperament is printed as unique, bold, and affectionate. Unique is the honest word. This is not a cuddly Amazon. The genus is its own, the display is its own, and the bite is not an Amazon pinch with a different paint job.",
    "When the bird is alarmed it raises a feather ruff, the fan of elongated nape feathers that gives the red-fan name. The crown lifts, the neck widens, and the bird looks larger and more hawk-like than it did on the perch a moment earlier. That display is a stress and threat signal. It is not a party trick and not a cue to clap, film, or push a hand closer so the fan opens again. A keeper who provokes the ruff to show guests is manufacturing the mood that leads to a bite.",
    "Hawk-heads can be nippy and unpredictable. Bold on the card is real. A bird that greets one person with interest may lunge at another, or at the same person on a different day, without a long warning that an Amazon owner would recognize. Affectionate applies to a bond built slowly with one careful handler. It does not describe a parrot that wants every visitor to scratch its neck. Unique means you cannot copy a caique or Amazon routine and expect the same result from Deroptyus.",
    "A patient handler is the minimum skill, not a decorative phrase. Step-up, a neutral perch, and an end to the session before the fan comes up are the tools. Punishment, surprise grabs, and crowded rooms make a nippy bird worse. Thirty to 40 years is the lifespan attached to that patience. A hawk-head bought as a first parrot often changes households after the first serious bite. The second household inherits a bird that has learned hands are a threat, which confirms the unpredictable label and was preventable.",
    "Moderate to loud is the noise line. The call is a harsh parrot voice, enough for a house and too much for a thin-walled apartment that wanted something picturesque. Moderate talking means some birds learn words and many use sound effects and contact calls instead. Do not buy Deroptyus as a talking project. Buy it, if at all, as a display species you are willing to read carefully for decades. South America is the origin. Captive-bred birds with a known history are the ones to consider. Wild-caught hawk-heads are a poor fit for the affectionate half of the temperament line.",
    "Housing is a strong parrot cage sized for a 12 to 14 inch bird that fans its neck and needs personal space, not a conure starter kit. Give a retreat so the bird can leave the front bars when the room is busy. Toys should occupy the beak without requiring the keeper to reach in past a bird that is already displaying. Foraging helps. So does a predictable schedule. Affection grows from that predictability. It does not grow from forcing a cuddle on a bold animal that has already lifted the ruff.",
    "Weight spans 6 to 10 ounces on this card, so two healthy adults can look quite different. Learn the individual bird's weight and appetite instead of guessing from chest shape alone. Diet is a standard parrot mix of pellets, vegetables, and a restrained nut portion. Seed as the whole bowl is as wrong here as it is for any large psittacine, and a hawk-head that feels ill becomes harder to handle, not easier. A quiet, fluffed bird that has stopped threatening is more worrying than one that still displays.",
    "Find an avian veterinarian before you need a bite wound managed on either side of the relationship. The handler's skin and the bird's stress are both part of this species. Annual exams, nail and beak checks, and a plan for restraint that does not turn into a wrestling match belong in the first month. Thirty to 40 years of improvising with a dog clinic is a bad match for a parrot this unusual. Take the paperwork, the species name, and a recent weight to the first visit so the clinic is not guessing which Amazon it has.",
    "People meet the fan in a photograph and expect a showy pet that likes attention. The bird behind the photograph is 12 to 14 inches, 6 to 10 ounces, moderate to loud, and only moderately talented at speech. It raises a feather ruff when alarmed. It can be nippy. It can also be affectionate with the person who earned that trust and did not spend it on performances. A patient handler with decades to give, and no need for an Amazon-shaped relationship, is the household that fits Deroptyus accipitrinus.",
  ],

  "birds/kakariki": [
    "Kakariki is the New Zealand name for parakeets in the genus Cyanoramphus. On this card they are 10 to 12 inches, 2 to 3 ounces, with a lifespan of 10 to 15 years. Origin is New Zealand. The noise level is moderate and the talking score is limited. Temperament is active, curious, and playful. That trio undersells the speed. These are extremely active parrots. A kakariki in a still cage, treated as a slightly fancy budgie, is a mismatched pet from the first afternoon.",
    "Several species share the name, including red-crowned and yellow-crowned birds, and they are not identical in status or in exact habits. Some Cyanoramphus species are protected in their native range. Pet birds should be captive-bred. A seller who cannot say which species is in the box, or who shrugs at paperwork, is selling a label rather than a kakariki. Ask for the scientific name. A 10 to 12 inch range already hints that the page covers more than one body, and the care has to fit the bird you actually received.",
    "Activity is the husbandry problem and the entertainment. Kakarikis run, climb, hang, and fly in short bursts that never quite become rest. Curious means every latch, plant, and cup handle gets tested. A safe room for daily flight is more important than a new toy. Fans, toilets, and gaps behind furniture are the accidents. Moderate noise is chirps, chatter, and flock contact, usually easier on neighbors than a conure scream and still constant when the bird is bored. Limited talking means you will not occupy this animal with speech lessons.",
    "They foot-play. Food, beads, and shreds of paper get held in a foot while the bird works them with the beak, often while hanging upside down. That habit is normal and worth encouraging with safe foot toys. It is also why cage design matters. Wide bar spacing, open chain loops, and frayed rope are toe traps. Bumblefoot and broken nails show up when the only perches are smooth plastic of one diameter. Offer natural wood of several thicknesses, and check the feet when you clean, because a 2 to 3 ounce bird will not announce a sore foot in words.",
    "Destructive is a fair description of the beak plus the curiosity. Baseboards, book spines, and live plants lose. The cage should already contain wood and paper the bird is allowed to ruin, replaced often, so the exploration has a legal target. A kakariki without that outlet spends the energy on the enclosure itself and then on its own feathers or feet. Playful is not a phase that ends after the first year. Across a 10 to 15 year life the bird remains a small demolition project with wings.",
    "Ten to 15 years is short beside a macaw and long enough to regret a tiny cage. People under-buy space because the weight is only 2 to 3 ounces. The length is 10 to 12 inches, much of it tail, and the activity multiplies the space requirement. A flight cage, or an aviary, fits better than a small parrot cage with a swing. Clipped wings on a bird this driven often produce a frustrated runner that still gets into trouble and can no longer get back up. Supervised flight is the kinder plan.",
    "Diet is a small-parrot mix: pellets the bird will actually eat, seed as a portion rather than the whole day, vegetables, and a little fruit. Foraging has to be easy to solve at first, because a curious kakariki that cannot crack the puzzle in a minute may simply leave it and go chew the stand. Weight changes are sudden in a 2 to 3 ounce body. A gram scale is more useful than a weekly glance. Fresh water in a tip-proof dish matters, since these birds bathe, splash, and then look for the next mess.",
    "New Zealand origin explains the outdoor-aviary tradition in their home country and the shock when a kakariki meets a heated apartment and a closed cage. Indoor birds still need daylight cycles, a bath, and hours of movement. They can live with a careful person and should not live with a loose dog or a cat that considers 2 ounces a toy. Other birds in the same cage are a case-by-case risk. Speed and nipping make them awkward roommates for calmer parrots. A pair that was raised together is a different question from two strangers.",
    "Limited speech, moderate noise, and a 10 to 15 year life are the numbers. The behavior those numbers sit on is extreme activity, foot play, and a destructive curiosity that some protected wild relatives never had to fold into a living room. Captive-bred stock is the responsible pet. A household that will let the bird fly, vary the perches, and replace shredded toys will see why keepers like them. A household that wanted a calm shoulder conure will see a blur, a chewed lamp cord, and a sore foot.",
  ],

  "birds/owl-finch": [
    "The owl finch is the double-barred finch, Taeniopygia bichenovii, a species also placed in Stizoptera. The face pattern suggests an owl. The animal is a small grassfinch, about 4 inches long, and it is not an owl in any sense that would change the husbandry. Lifespan is 5 to 8 years. Noise is very low. Care is intermediate. These birds are kept as pairs or small groups in a flight, watched rather than handled. They are not hand-tame parrots, and no amount of finger training turns them into one.",
    "The cage minimum is 24 by 12 by 18 inches, with a flight cage preferred. Horizontal distance matters more than height. A pair needs room to pass, to roost together, and to get away from the glass when the room is loud. Drafts, kitchens, and cages parked in a hallway wear down a 4-inch bird. The diet is seeds, live insects, and greens. A finch mix plus a little egg food in molt or breeding, and brief offerings of safe greens, is the practical menu. Live food is a supplement. It is not an excuse to fill the cage with large worms the birds cannot eat.",
    "Very low noise is the correct expectation: soft contact notes, not a song that fills the flat, and not speech. Five to 8 years is still a real commitment for a bird that was cheap and looks delicate. Intermediate care is the pair bond, the temperature, clean seed, and a daily check that both birds are eating and perched. A single owl finch does not become more tame from loneliness. It becomes a stressed grassfinch. Larger, pushier finches can bully them, so a mixed flight has to be watched. They startle upward. A bare low roof and a sudden hand are how these birds injure themselves. Keep the group, skip the handling demonstrations, and treat a bird on the floor as a medical problem the same day.",
  ],

  "birds/plum-headed-parakeet": [
    "The plum-headed parakeet is Psittacula cyanocephala, a parrot from the Indian subcontinent listed at 13 inches and 2.5 to 3 ounces. Adult males have the plum-colored head. Birds without that head may be hens or young males, so a grey-headed juvenile is not automatically a different species and not automatically female. The voice is rated low, and talking ability on the card is moderate. Temperament is gentle, quiet, and social. Low and quiet are real next to an Alexandrine, and they do not turn this bird into a decoration that can live in a budgie cage.",
    "Quieter than an Alexandrine is the useful comparison. Thirteen inches and a few ounces is a slimmer Psittacula than P. eupatria, and the call is easier to live with. It is still a parrot call. Apartments that cannot bear any flock noise will hear this one too, just not at Alexandrine volume. Moderate talking means some males learn words and phrases. Speech is a pleasant extra, not the reason to skip foraging and time out of the cage. Social birds want a person or a well-matched partner. A covered cage in a spare room is not social.",
    "Lifespan is 15 to 20 years. That is shorter than a large macaw and long enough to outgrow a starter cage bought in a hurry. Out-of-cage time is a daily need, in a room you have checked for fans, open doors, and metals the beak should not find. The long tail needs perch placement that does not force the feathers into a corner. Gentle birds bite when they are hormonal or grabbed. The bite is smaller than an Alexandrine's and still a poor fit for a child's unsupervised hand.",
    "Diet is pellets and vegetables, with seed and a little fruit as the smaller share. At 2.5 to 3 ounces, a sunflower habit puts fat on a bird that looks fine until it is not. Offer greens the bird will actually try, cut small, and rotate them. Bathing keeps the plum head and the body feathers in order. A shallow dish or a light mist, not a blast of cold water, is enough. Weight on a gram scale tells you whether the quiet bird is quiet because it is content or because it is ill.",
    "Indian subcontinent origin sits on the card for the wild range. Pets should be captive-bred. A male in full plum head, a low voice, and a moderate gift for words are the attractions. The obligations are a Psittacula-sized cage, daily freedom in a safe room, and 15 to 20 years of a diet that is not a seed stick. Quiet on the temperament line means you can hold a conversation in the same room. It does not mean the bird can be left without company, without flight, or without a veterinarian who sees parrots.",
  ],

  "birds/princess-parrot": [
    "The princess parrot is Polytelis alexandrae, a long-tailed Australian parrot listed at 18 inches and only 4 to 5 ounces. Much of that length is tail. The body is slim, which is why people mistake the bird for something closer to a budgie with extra feathers. It is not a budgie. The noise rating is low, and words are listed as limited. Temperament is gentle, quiet, and elegant. Those words describe a shy, flying parrot, not a shoulder companion that chats from a collar.",
    "In the wild, princess parrots are nomadic, moving through arid Australian country as conditions change. Captive birds do not need a desert, and they do need a long flight cage. Eighteen inches of tail and wing cannot be housed in a small parrot cage without crushing the point of the species. Horizontal flight, several perches placed so the tail clears, and daily time in a safe room match a nomadic flyer better than a round cage and a mirror. Elegant fades quickly when the tail is broken against the bars.",
    "Lifespan is 15 to 25 years. Limited talking means words are uncommon and should not be the purchase reason. The low noise rating is one of the real advantages: a household can keep a princess parrot without the dawn scream of a conure. Quiet is not the same as interactive on human terms. Many birds remain reserved, more willing to fly to a familiar perch than to cuddle. Forcing shoulder time on a gentle Polytelis produces a bird that avoids hands. Watching a long flight is the better relationship.",
    "Australia is the origin. Captive-bred birds are the pets to consider. Diet should be pellets, seed in a controlled portion, vegetables, and greens, not a millet-only budgie plan stretched to 18 inches. Four to 5 ounces is a light bird for the length, and it does not give you room to ignore weight loss. A gram scale, a look at the keel, and droppings on the cage paper are the home checks. An avian veterinarian still has to be lined up, because low noise can hide a sick bird that a loud species would have announced by going silent.",
    "A long flight cage is the equipment decision that separates a fair setup from a disappointed one. Aviaries suit them. A living-room cage built for a cockatiel does not. Gentle, quiet, and elegant are enjoyable in a household that likes to watch a parrot fly and can accept limited speech for 15 to 25 years. They are a poor match for someone who wanted a budgie's price and a conure's demand to be held. Give the tail somewhere to go, keep the diet adult, and let the nomadic history explain why a still, small cage goes wrong.",
  ],

  "birds/red-factor-canary": [
    "A red-factor canary is a color breed of the domestic canary, about 5 inches long, with a lifespan of 10 to 15 years. The red is genetic, and it shows in the feathers only when the diet supplies carotenoid pigment while those feathers are growing. It is not a dye painted on, and a bird that was reddened with a marker or a bath will not pass that color at the next molt. The noise line is moderate. Care level is beginner. The diet line is seeds, color food, and greens. Color food is the carotenoid source. It does not replace the seeds and the greens.",
    "This is not an American Singer. Singers are judged on song and were developed from roller and border-type birds. Red-factor birds are judged, when they are shown, on color and type. A household can enjoy both breeds and should not buy one while expecting the other's scorecard. Males sing. A red male in breeding condition will sing at a level the card calls moderate, which is pleasant in a room and tiring if the cage sits beside a bed with no night cover. Hens are usually the quieter sex. Buy the sex you mean to house if song is part of the decision.",
    "Color food belongs in the molt, when the new feather is being made, and in the weeks around that molt according to the product's plain instructions. It does not belong as a year-round substitute for vegetables, and it is not a medicine. Skip any plan that involves painting, dipping, or oiling the finished feather. Greens and a quality seed mix, with egg food during the molt, keep the bird in condition so the carotenoids have a healthy feather to color. A thin, fluffed red canary with intense color is not a success. It is a sick 5-inch bird.",
    "A cage of 24 by 12 by 18 inches is the listed minimum, and a flight cage is what keeps a color canary moving between perches. Draft-free placement matters as much as the color food. A show cage is a temporary box, not a home. Perches of more than one diameter, a cuttlebone or mineral block, and a bath dish a few times a week cover the body that has to grow the next red molt. Kitchens add fumes these birds do not tolerate. Direct afternoon sun through a closed window overheats a small cage.",
    "Ten to 15 years covers many molts. The red will rise and fall with health, molt quality, and whether carotenoids were actually in the food while the feather was open. After the molt, extra color feeding does not repaint a finished wing. Plan the diet before the feathers drop, not after you dislike the new ones. Store color food as the label says, and throw out seed that is dusty, musty, or full of husks the bird has already sorted through. Empty calories and stale mix produce a pale, itchy bird at the next cycle.",
    "Beginner is a fair care level for a single male or a hen kept as a song-and-color pet, provided the night is quiet and the food is consistent. Breeding for a deeper red is a separate hobby, with genetics, extra cages, and a tolerance for chicks that do not all match the parents. Moderate noise is male song, not speech. Nobody should buy a red-factor canary expecting words. They should expect a 5-inch domestic canary, a 10 to 15 year life, a flight cage, and a molt diet that treats carotenoids as nutrition rather than as paint.",
  ],

  "birds/red-rumped-parrot": [
    "The red-rumped parrot is Psephotus haematonotus, an Australian grass parrot listed at 11 inches and 2 to 3 ounces. The male has the red rump the species is named for. Hens are plainer and greener, which leads shops to split a pair and sell the bright bird as the pet and the dull bird as optional. They do better as a pair in a flight than as a shoulder bird. The card marks noise as low and talking as limited. Temperament is calm, hardy, and independent. Independent is the word that argues against a cuddle-pet plan.",
    "A flight, or a long cage that actually lets them fly, matches a grass parrot. An 11-inch bird in a small parrot cage, asked to sit on a finger, is the setup most likely to disappoint. Calm does not mean stationary. Hardy does not mean the pair can live on seed cake and a swing. Low noise is a genuine advantage over rosellas and conures, and limited talking means you should not expect phrases. The pleasure is watching a pair, the male's red rump included, move the length of a flight.",
    "A red-rump's listed life is 15 to 20 years. That is a long time to keep a bird in the wrong social arrangement. Pairs do well. A single hand-tame male sometimes bonds to a person and still is not a shoulder parrot in the conure sense. Breeding season can make even a calm pair aggressive toward other birds and, at times, toward hands near the nest box. Give them a flight of their own rather than a mixed aviary with no way to separate fighters. Australia on the origin line is the wild range. Buy captive-bred birds.",
    "Diet in a flight is a grass-parrot mix: a measured seed base, greens, and vegetables, with pellets if the birds will take them. Two to 3 ounces does not leave room for a sunflower habit. Scatter some of the food and hang greens so the pair works for the meal the way they would on the ground. Clean water at ground level and at a higher bowl gives a choice. Bathing is common and soaks the floor under the flight, which is a reason to plan the room, not a behavior problem.",
    "Hardy is why people recommend red-rumps to aviary keepers, and it is also why neglect lasts longer than it should before anyone notices. Check the red rump and the hen's weight, not just the cheerful movement. A bird on the floor of the flight, fluffed, is the one to catch and have seen. Limited speech will not tell you which partner is being driven off the food. Fifteen to 20 years of a low, busy pair in a real flight is the good version of this species. A single bird on a shoulder, in a living-room cage, is the version the species is worse at.",
  ],

  "birds/rosellas": [
    "Rosella on this page is a group of Australian parakeets in the genus Platycercus, not a single pet with one temperament. The eastern rosella, Platycercus eximius, is the common pet. The card lists 12 to 14 inches, 3 to 5 ounces, a lifespan of 15 to 25 years, moderate noise, and limited talking. Temperament is active, independent, and beautiful. Beautiful is a plumage fact. Independent is the behavior fact. These are flying birds with opinions about other birds, not cuddly shoulder parrots in a brighter coat.",
    "Eastern rosellas are the ones most buyers mean. Crimson, pale-headed, and other Platycercus species turn up under the same shop word and do not share every habit. Ask which rosella is in the cage before you trust a 12 to 14 inch label as a complete description. All of them need a long flight. A small parrot cage, the sort sold for a cockatiel, is the wrong room for a 12 to 14 inch bird with a long tail and a need to fly. Clipped wings in that small cage remove the behavior the group is built around.",
    "They can be aggressive with other birds. A pair of eastern rosellas in their own flight is a standard plan. The same pair in a living-room cage with cockatiels, or with a second rosella pair, is how birds get scalped feet and broken skin. Even a bonded pair may need separation in breeding condition. Moderate noise is chatter and flock calls, louder than a red-rump and easier than a conure, and still enough to hear through an open window. Limited talking means speech is not a feature to shop for.",
    "Origin on the card is Australia. Captive-bred birds from aviaries are the normal pets. Diet should be varied: seed as part of the day, greens, vegetables, and pellets if they accept them. Active birds in a flight burn more food than a perched pet and still get fat if the bowl is nothing but sunflower. Hang food in more than one place so the subordinate bird eats. Fifteen to 25 years is the lifespan, so a flight built as a temporary pen becomes a decade of the wrong housing if it is too short to fly.",
    "Independent and active describe a rosella that would rather cover the length of an aviary than sit for a scratch. Some hand-raised eastern rosellas tolerate a familiar person. They remain a poor match for anyone who wants a cuddly pet parrot. Beautiful plumage does not change the aggression risk with other species. Give Platycercus eximius, or whichever rosella you actually bought, a long flight of its own, a varied diet, and 15 to 25 years of moderate noise. Leave the small shared parrot cage out of the plan.",
  ],

  "birds/toucan": [
    "Toucan on this page is a softbill group, not one species. The card says large, 18 to 25 inches, with a lifespan of 15 to 20 years, moderate noise, a diet of fruit, pellets, and insects, advanced care, and a cage minimum of 24 by 24 by 24 inches. That size range already refuses to be a single bird. A keeper has to know which toucan they have, because a toucanet and a toco are different sizes. A cube that is a printed minimum for the group is not a serious plan for every body that shops call a toucan.",
    "Name the species before you trust the cage math. A small toucanet and a toco do not share a flight length, a perch diameter, or a food budget, even if both are sold as toucans. Eighteen to 25 inches is the span printed here. Use it as a warning that the page is generic, then look up the adult length of the bird in front of you. Advanced care starts with that identification. It does not start with a decorative bill and a fruit bowl in a parrot cage.",
    "Toucans and toucanets eat a low-iron fruit diet. The pellets on the card have to be a low-iron softbill formula, not a high-iron parrot pellet poured into a fruit dish because the shape looks familiar. Iron storage disease is the husbandry problem attached to that mistake. Too much citrus interacts with iron uptake and is part of the same warning, so fruit is not an unlimited orange-and-tomato menu. Insects and a planned fruit mix belong in the diet. A seed hopper does not. Water should be clean, and the food has to be replaced often because cut fruit spoils.",
    "They are loud, messy, and not apartment birds. The card's moderate noise rating still describes a call that carries, and the bill throws fruit, then droppings, farther than a parrot of similar length. Walls, floors, and anyone walking under the perch take the mess. A 24 by 24 by 24 inch minimum is already a tight cube for a bird at the long end of 18 to 25 inches, and it does nothing about the splatter zone outside the bars. Housing is a large aviary-style cage in a room you are willing to wash, or it is the wrong species.",
    "Fifteen to 20 years is the lifespan on an advanced animal that needs a specialist diet from the first week. High-iron pellets fed for convenience are not a small error you can average out later. Find a veterinarian who has seen ramphastids before the bird arrives, and bring the species name, not the word toucan alone. Weight, foot condition, and the look of the droppings matter because the bill hides a body that can be thinner than strangers expect. A bird that sits fluffed behind a huge bill is sick, whatever the pet-shop sign promised.",
    "Moderate noise, advanced care, and a fruit-pellet-insect diet are the printed facts. The practical reading is a loud, messy softbill whose cage must match the species, whose pellets must be low in iron, and whose fruit bowl cannot lean on citrus. A toucanet and a toco are not interchangeable 18 to 25 inch pets, and a 15 to 20 year life is long enough to regret guessing. If the receipt does not name the bird, you do not yet know which animal you are feeding or which flight it will need when it is grown.",
  ],
};
